      <h2>Mistakes to avoid when picking {verticalLower} software</h2>

      <h3>1. Buying for the team you have, not the one you plan to have</h3>
      <p>
        If you plan to hire, check that the tool handles dispatching and team management before
        you need them. Switching platforms later means moving job history, customer records and
        recurring service agreements, so ask each vendor what its import and export carry over.
        <strong>Buy for the team size you plan to have, not only the one you have today.</strong>
      </p>

      <h3>2. Underestimating implementation time</h3>
      <p>
        Setup time depends on how much customer data and how many recurring contracts you bring
        over, and on how much training your team needs. Ask each vendor for a written onboarding
        plan, and don't sign a contract that locks you into a go-live date you can't realistically hit.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Skipping the QuickBooks integration deep-dive</h3>
      <p>
        {qbCount} of the {ranked.length} {toolsWord} on this page {qbCount === 1 ? 'lists' : 'list'} a QuickBooks integration. If you
        keep your books in QuickBooks, verify these before signing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>QuickBooks Desktop vs Online support</strong>: confirm the tool supports the version your accountant uses; support for one does not mean support for the other.</li>
        <li><strong>Sync direction</strong>: one-way push (FSM → QuickBooks) vs two-way sync. Two-way matters if your accountant adjusts entries in QB.</li>
        <li><strong>Sync frequency</strong>: ask whether changes sync in real time or on a schedule. This matters more if you reconcile daily than if you reconcile weekly.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer record merge behavior</strong>: when a customer exists in both systems with slightly different addresses, ask whether the tool merges the records or creates a duplicate.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4. Over-indexing on price at the entry tier</h3>
      <p>
        Check what the entry tier leaves out (for example dispatch optimization, automated
        reminders or recurring service tracking) before you compare prices.
        Compare the tier you'd realistically use, not the marketing "starting at" price.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Ignoring the team-adoption tax</h3>
      <p>
        Software only pays off if the team uses it. A polished app doesn't help if your
        senior tech won't enter job notes in it. Before committing, get your most
        software-resistant team members on a demo. If they push back hard on basic workflows,
        keep shopping.
      </p>

